course: world
lesson: 75
title: Mammals and how they care for their young
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: All mammals feed their babies with milk made in the mother's body, so every baby mammal needs its mother at the start of its life.
explain: Most mammals grow inside the mother and are born alive; marsupials such as kangaroos are born tiny and keep growing in the mother's pouch; and a few mammals, the platypus and the echidnas, lay eggs but still feed milk.
explain: Some babies, like foals and baby giraffes, are born with open eyes and can walk within hours; others, like kittens, puppies and human babies, are born helpless and need long care.
explain: Parents keep the young warm, clean and safe, and they teach them: a lioness shows her cubs how to hunt, and a young elephant learns where to find water from the older females of its herd.
explain: A newborn kangaroo is about the size of a jelly bean; it crawls into its mother's pouch, drinks milk there for months, and only then hops out into the world.
fact: All mammals feed their young with milk.
fact: Mammals have hair or fur on their bodies.
fact: Most mammals give birth to live young.
fact: Marsupials, such as kangaroos and koalas, carry their tiny newborns in a pouch.
fact: The platypus and the echidnas are mammals that lay eggs.
fact: A newborn foal can stand and walk within a few hours of birth.
fact: A foal must walk soon so it can keep up with the herd and run from danger.
fact: Kittens and puppies are born with their eyes closed.
fact: Human babies need care from adults for many years, longer than almost any other animal.
fact: An elephant mother carries her baby for about 22 months before it is born.
fact: In an elephant herd, the older females help to look after the calves.
fact: Young mammals learn many skills, such as hunting, by watching and copying their parents.
fact: A blue whale calf drinks a huge amount of milk and puts on around 90 kilograms a day.
exercise: What do all mammal mothers feed their babies? | Milk from their own bodies | Insects they catch for them | Food they chew and spit out | Nothing until the babies can hunt
exercise: How are most mammal babies born? | They are born alive | They hatch from hard eggs | They hatch from soft eggs in water | They grow from seeds in a nest
exercise: Where does a baby kangaroo grow after it is born? | In its mother's pouch | In a nest of grass | In an underground burrow | On its father's back
exercise: Which mammal lays eggs? | The platypus | The kangaroo | The dolphin | The bat
exercise: Why is the platypus called a mammal even though it lays eggs? | It has fur and feeds its young milk | It lives in rivers and swims very well underwater | It has a beak like a duck's beak | It lays its eggs in a burrow
exercise: Which baby can walk a few hours after it is born? | A foal | A kitten | A human baby | A puppy
exercise: How are kittens born? | With closed eyes, needing their mother | With open eyes, ready to hunt straight away | Inside eggs that the mother sits on to warm | Able to find their own food from the first day
exercise: How big is a newborn kangaroo? | About the size of a jelly bean | About the size of a cat | About the size of a football or bigger | About the size of its mother
exercise: Who helps an elephant mother look after her calf? | The older females of the herd | The calf's father alone | Nobody, the calf lives all alone from birth | Birds that sit on its back
exercise: How does a lion cub learn to hunt? | By watching and copying its mother | It knows everything from birth | By reading the smells of old meat | It never hunts, it only eats grass
exercise: Which animal looks after its young for the longest time? | Humans | Mice | Rabbits | Horses
exercise: Why does a foal need to walk so soon after birth? | To keep up with the herd and escape danger | Because its mother has no milk | Because it lives alone from the start and needs no mother | To find a nest to hatch in
exercise: Why do baby mammals need to stay close to their mother at first? | They need her milk to live | They need her to lay their eggs | They need her to teach them to breathe | They need her to grow their fur
exercise: How long does an elephant mother carry her baby before birth? | About 22 months | About 2 weeks | About 9 days | About 3 years
test: A farmer finds a newborn calf. What will it need to drink in its first days? | Its mother's milk | Clean pond water | Fresh orange juice | Nothing for several weeks
test: Which is true about mammals? | Every mammal feeds its young with milk | Every mammal is born from an egg | Every mammal lives on land | Every mammal is born able to walk and run at once
test: A scientist finds an animal with fur that lays eggs and feeds its babies milk. What is it? | An egg-laying mammal like a platypus | A bird that has grown fur instead of feathers | A reptile that makes milk for its babies | A fish that has learned to live on land
test: Why do kittens stay in a safe nest for the first weeks? | They are born helpless with closed eyes | They are too heavy to move about on their own | Their mother forbids them to see light | They are waiting to hatch
test: What would happen to a baby kangaroo if it could not reach the pouch? | It could not drink milk and would not survive | It would simply grow up faster outside its mother's body | Its father would feed it with insects | It would turn into a different animal
test: Which pair shows a helpless baby and a ready-to-walk baby? | A puppy and a baby giraffe | A foal and a baby zebra | A baby giraffe and a foal | A kitten and a puppy
test: Why is a long childhood useful for human children? | It gives time to learn a great deal | It keeps them small and light for longer | It means they need no food | It stops them from getting ill
test: A young elephant that has lost its mother joins another herd. Who is most likely to look after it? | The older females of the new herd | The young males of the herd | A lion that finds it | Nobody, elephants never help others
test: How does a mammal mother differ from a frog mother? | She feeds her young milk and cares for them | She lays thousands of eggs in water | She leaves her young straight after birth | Her babies breathe with gills at first
test: why are puppies born with their eyes shut | Kittens and puppies are born with eyes closed and open them later | Their mum closes them to keep out the light | They are blind for life until they are washed | They keep them shut to save energy for growing
test: how come a baby horse can walk straight away | A newborn foal can stand and walk within a few hours of birth | Foals walk because their mum pushes them along | Foals are born much older than other babies | Foals learn to walk while still inside their mum
test: Mia says all mammals have live babies. true? | No, the platypus and echidnas are mammals that lay eggs | Yes, no mammal ever lays an egg | Yes, but some of them carry eggs in a pouch | No, most mammals lay eggs in nests
test: my mate reckons a kangaroo baby is born big and jumps into the pouch. right? | No, it is born tiny and grows in its mother's pouch | Yes, it is born nearly fully grown | Yes, and then it leaves the pouch the same day | No, kangaroos lay eggs inside the pouch
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the feature common to all mammals? | They feed their young with milk | They give birth to live young without exception | They carry their young in a pouch | They lay eggs with hard shells
exam: Identify, from the animals hereinafter enumerated, that mammal which lays eggs. | The platypus | The kangaroo | The koala | The elephant
exam: Ascertain the approximate period for which an elephant mother carries her young before birth. | About 22 months | About 9 months | About 6 months | About 40 months
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the acquisition of skills by young mammals. | Many are learned by watching and copying their parents | All are possessed at birth without any learning | They are learned solely from other species | They are acquired only after the parents depart
